const entries = {
  'ecoset': {
    bCls: 'badge-fore', bLabel: 'Forerunner',
    meta: 'Los Angeles · Founded 2009',
    name: 'EcoSet',
    editorial: 'The Proof of Durability',
    stats: [
      ['Founded','2009, from a 2008 prototype on a Coen Brothers feature, A Serious Man, in Minneapolis'],
      ['Founder','Shannon Schaefer Bart (now Senior Manager, Global Production Sustainability, Netflix)'],
      ['Executive Director','Kris Barberg (joined 2009, partner since 2012)'],
      ['Headquarters','Materials Oasis, 3019 Andrita St, Glassell Park, Los Angeles 90065'],
      ['Waste diverted','2,973 tons (EcoSet dashboard, May 2026)'],
      ['Resources donated','$11+ million in reusable materials (May 2026)'],
      ['Bottles avoided','682,000+ (May 2026)'],
      ['Status','Active. Women-owned. WBENC-certified since 2013.']
    ],
    body: [
      `What Shannon Schaefer Bart saw on film sets had no department name in 2008. There was no Eco Crew function, no sustainability protocol, no standard procedure for what happened to the materials a production left behind at wrap. Sets went to dumpsters. Food went to trash. Office supplies, furniture, lumber, and costumes went wherever the art department could arrange in the final chaotic days of a shoot, which usually meant nowhere useful. The industry had no mechanism to ask the question, because the question had never been made part of the job.`,
      `What Bart and Barberg built on A Serious Man in Minneapolis was a proof of concept for something that did not yet have a name. Over ten production weeks, the experiment diverted eleven tons of waste from incineration. Office supplies and furniture went to schools and nonprofits across the Twin Cities. Sets and construction materials went to independent filmmakers rather than into the waste stream. What they built next, in Los Angeles starting in 2009, was a new on-set department: not a consulting add-on, not a green initiative handed down from the studio, but an Eco Crew embedded in the production itself, working from pre-production planning through the final wrap day.`,
      `The years since have produced a particular kind of operational wisdom. The company survived COVID, when zero-waste targets that had reached ninety-percent landfill diversion dropped to fifty or seventy percent almost overnight as health rules pushed single-use plastics back across the industry. The adaptation, per Barberg's account to Sustainable Brands in July 2024, was to add logistics capabilities, diversify revenue, rent out warehouse space, and stay nimble. The Materials Oasis, EcoSet's physical reuse center in Glassell Park, is what the circular-economy idea looks like made concrete: nonprofits, schools, artists, theaters, and filmmakers come in by appointment and browse production discards at no cost. It won the Los Angeles County Board of Supervisors Green Leadership Award in 2017. Partnerships include TreePeople, with five trees planted per commercial shoot, and the Boys &amp; Girls Club.`,
      `There is a vindication in where the founder went next. Bart created a role that did not exist, and the industry's largest engines came looking for it. She built the NBCUniversal Sustainable Production Program, implementing it across film and television worldwide, and is now Senior Manager of Global Production Sustainability at Netflix. The function she invented in a Minneapolis production office in 2008, the one with no name and no precedent, is now staffed at the top of the industry by the person who named it.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"2024 marks 15 years in business. We've honed our resilient business model based on adaptation and diversification, staying nimble and pivoting quickly. We survived 2020 by adding more logistics capabilities to our wheelhouse and renting out space."`, attr: 'Kris Barberg, Executive Director, EcoSet, Sustainable Brands, July 2024', color: 'gold' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-film', label: 'Why this matters', text: `Two women, with no studio behind them, built one of the first real operations for reclaiming what a film production throws away, an embedded crew, a warehouse, a standing department where before there had only been a dumpster. Anyone can found a good idea. The rare thing, the thing that makes EcoSet the proof, is that they kept it running, through a pandemic that gutted the industry overnight, since 2009. Almost no one in this field has earned that word the way they have.` }
  },
  'earth-angel': {
    bCls: 'badge-fore', bLabel: 'Forerunner',
    meta: 'Brooklyn · Founded 2013, merged 2024',
    name: 'Earth Angel',
    editorial: 'The East Coast Pioneer',
    stats: [
      ['Founded',"2013, Brooklyn, by Emellie O'Brien"],
      ['Acquired by','TheGreenShot (Brussels-based, founded 2021), May 29, 2024'],
      ['Current status','Standalone Earth Angel brand retired; O\'Brien now Chief Impact Manager of the merged group'],
      ['Productions','180+ (2013-2024 standalone era)'],
      ['Carbon avoided','29,000+ metric tons (Earth Angel\'s own figure, through 2024)'],
      ['Bottles avoided','6 million+ single-use plastic (Earth Angel\'s own figure, through 2024)'],
      ['Notable work','The Amazing Spider-Man 2 (2014), Noah (2014), The Whale (2022)'],
      ['Recognition','Tory Burch Foundation Fellow 2016; Inc. 30 Under 30 2018']
    ],
    body: [
      `Emellie O'Brien graduated from NYU's Tisch film program and walked onto her first professional film set in 2011. What she saw there, the cases of single-use plastic water bottles, the dumpsters full of struck set at wrap, the food going straight from craft services to the garbage, became the rest of her career. She took her first sustainability role with Big Beach Films that same year, a job that mostly meant sixteen-hour days digging through set trash. The crew, watching her sort recycling from compost over the walkie, started calling her Earth Angel. In 2013 she turned the nickname into a company.`,
      `The engagement that made the company's reputation came in 2014. Earth Angel's sustainability leadership on The Amazing Spider-Man 2 helped earn Sony Pictures recognition for the most eco-friendly blockbuster in the studio's history, and the production became the first to carry the Sony Pictures a Greener World end credit. Doug Belgrad, then president of Columbia Pictures, said the team's practices saved more than four hundred thousand dollars. The work was specific and measurable: nearly fifty tons of material kept for donation or reuse, a hundred ninety thousand disposable water bottles avoided, a fifty-two percent landfill diversion rate, thousands of meals donated, and tree planting in a Sandy-damaged stretch of New York's East River Park.`,
      `Darren Aronofsky worked with Earth Angel on Noah the same year and put it in a filmmaker's terms: 'When we did Noah, we knew we were making a film about the first steward of the earth, so we wanted to be good stewards ourselves. There's so much waste on film sets, and because of groups like Earth Angel, we were able to change that a little bit.' He came back to work with her on The Whale in 2022, where the production diverted eighty-two percent of its waste and gifted its stage flats to a local performing-arts academy.`,
      `In May 2014, one year into Earth Angel's existence, Eva Radke of Film Biz Recycling presented O'Brien with the Founder's Award for an Individual Displaying Utter Greatness, at the first Golden Dumpster Awards in Brooklyn. Film Biz Recycling's own Brooklyn warehouse would close in less than twelve months, though the work itself would move and carry on elsewhere. The torch was moving between two Brooklyn operators, visibly, in public, in front of the people who had built this field. O'Brien had in fact discovered Film Biz Recycling a few years earlier, while researching a green initiative for Focus Features, the older operation teaching the newer one before either had a name for what they shared.`,
      `On May 29, 2024, after eleven years and more than a hundred eighty productions, Earth Angel was acquired by TheGreenShot, a Brussels-based company, founded 2021, that folds financial, operational, and environmental tracking for productions into a single platform. The standalone Earth Angel brand was retired. The site that once booked its work now reads, simply, that after an incredible journey to make productions more sustainable, Earth Angel has closed. O'Brien continues inside the merged group as its Chief Impact Manager. The work she built goes on. The name does not, and she chose that trade herself.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"I knew what a powerful medium this was and really wanted to help leverage it toward educating folks on these important issues. It wasn't until I got onto my first professional film set where my eyes were really opened to the amount of resource inefficiency that was happening."`, attr: "Emellie O'Brien, Earth Angel founder, Deadline, May 2024", color: 'gold' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-film', label: 'Why this matters', text: `Emellie O'Brien walked onto a film set, saw the waste no one was accounting for, and did the rarest thing in this lineage: she invented the job that did not exist, then built the company around it, and became the East Coast's defining operator in a field she helped name. What makes her story unusual is its ending. Earth Angel did not collapse, and its founder was not quietly hired away. After more than a decade of proving the work, O'Brien folded it into something larger by choice, trading a beloved brand for a longer reach. Lasting a decade is harder than shining for a few years, and choosing your own ending is its own kind of achievement.` }
  },
  'rawk': {
    bCls: 'badge-fore', bLabel: 'Forerunner',
    meta: 'Cedarhurst, New York · Founded 1991',
    name: 'Rock and Wrap It Up!',
    editorial: 'The Senior Operator',
    stats: [
      ['Founded','1991 informally (food recovery at Jones Beach Theatre); launched nationally 1994 via MTV'],
      ['Founders','Syd Mandelbaum and Diane Mandelbaum'],
      ['Founder & CEO','Syd Mandelbaum (still active, age 75)'],
      ['COO','Diane Mandelbaum (co-founder; runs operations, finance, and fundraising)'],
      ['Type','501(c)(3) anti-poverty think tank'],
      ['Headquarters','405 Oceanpoint Ave, Cedarhurst, NY (Long Island)'],
      ['Impact',"Organization states 1 billion+ fed since 1991 (RWU's own framing); EPA documented 100M+ lbs to 200M+ people as of 2009"],
      ['Donor categories','Touring bands, 70+ pro sports franchises, schools, hospitals, hospitality, TV/film including NBCUniversal'],
      ['Motivation',"To honor Mandelbaum's parents, Holocaust survivors"],
      ['Federal law','Drafted language that became the basis of the Federal Food Donation Act of 2008'],
      ['Status','Active']
    ],
    body: [
      `Syd Mandelbaum is a scientist by profession. He earned a BS and an MA from Brooklyn College and an MBA from Hofstra, served on the Lawrence school board and as a Nassau County Human Rights Commissioner, and built a career in DNA and genetics research. In 1994 he headed the American team that used DNA to disprove a claim to be the lost Grand Duchess Anastasia, described as the first use of DNA for a historical question. He later co-founded the DNA Shoah Project, which uses forensic DNA to reconnect families separated by the Holocaust, and in the early 1980s was among the first to videotape Holocaust survivor testimony. Rock and Wrap It Up! is the work he is best known for, and he and his wife Diane started it to honor his parents, both survivors. The specific spark was a June 1981 trip to Jerusalem with his father Joseph for the first World Gathering of Jewish Holocaust Survivors, after which, he has said, he felt he had to go home and change something.`,
      `It began with a phone call and a soup kitchen. In 1991 Mandelbaum was president of the Claddagh Inn soup kitchen in Rockaway Beach when the concert promoter Ron Delsener told him there was untouched food at the Jones Beach Theatre every summer and he was welcome to it. That first summer they recovered more than four thousand pounds. The insight that followed was logistical, not moral: the impulse to donate surplus food already existed everywhere, but it depended on the right person being in the room. A contractual mechanism could make it happen reliably, at every show, even when no one in the room thought to ask.`,
      `That mechanism became the contractual rider, and the organization launched nationally in 1994. The artists who signed first, among them Bruce Hornsby and the Range, the Rolling Stones, Aerosmith, Phish, and Michael Bolton, were not writing checks. They were signing contracts that obligated the venues to release prepared but unserved food at the end of each show. Goodwill was replaced with obligation. Over the following decades the model extended outward from music to professional sports, more than seventy franchises now, and to the hospitality industry, schools, hospitals, and film and television productions, with NBCUniversal among the named entertainment partners. The organization's film and television work runs under Project: It's a Wrap!, which began in 1997, the first film being The Devil's Own.`,
      `How much the model has recovered is harder to state cleanly than the organization's headline suggests. Rock and Wrap It Up! says it has fed more than a billion people since 1991, a figure it frames inconsistently across its own materials as people, meals, and pounds. The most recent independently documented numbers are the Environmental Protection Agency's, which in 2009 credited the organization with more than a hundred million pounds of food delivered to more than two hundred million people across five hundred cities, run by roughly six employees on a budget under half a million dollars a year. The Whole Earth Calculator, launched with the NFL at the 2014 Super Bowl, converts pounds of food diverted into meals served and greenhouse gas emissions avoided in a single calculation. Mandelbaum, now in his seventies, is still CEO. He was photographed in 2020 personally unloading food at a Long Island community center during the worst of the COVID year. His reach went past the loading dock and into the law: the language he drafted, working with Representative Jo Ann Emerson and Senator Chuck Schumer, became the basis of the Federal Food Donation Act of 2008, which pushed the federal government to give away its own surplus food rather than trash it.`,
      `The lineage shows up on film sets directly. Earth Angel, Emellie O'Brien's sustainability company, partnered with Rock and Wrap It Up! on the set of Annie in 2014, redistributing thousands of pounds of catering food through the model Mandelbaum had spent two decades building. In Los Angeles today, EcoSet channels craft services and catering food through Every Day Action, a separate operation reaching the same end by the same logic. The organizations are not linked, but the instinct is one and the same: build the mechanism, and the food finds the people who need it.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"I came up with something called contractual obligation, which is still used today. We thought that a band's management could put a stipulation in their rider, saying that all edible leftover food, prepared but not served, cannot go into a landfill. It must feed the hungry."`, attr: 'Syd Mandelbaum, Founder and CEO, Rock and Wrap It Up!, MUSE (Members United for Sustainable Events), 2022', color: 'gold' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-film', label: 'Why this matters', text: `Surplus food was always being given away here and there, whenever the right person happened to be in the room. Syd Mandelbaum's contribution was to remove the chance. He turned goodwill into a contractual obligation: a rider that requires a venue to release its uneaten food, every show, whether or not anyone thinks to ask. That single move is what made food recovery a system instead of a favor, and thirty-five years on, every operator doing this work on a film set is still building inside what he proved. The impulse was never rare. The mechanism was.` }
  },
  'fbr': {
    bCls: 'badge-fore', bLabel: 'Forerunner',
    meta: 'Brooklyn · Founded 2008 · Moved 2015 (still running today)',
    name: 'Film Biz Recycling',
    editorial: 'The Lightning Point',
    provenance: 'Built from a conversation with Eva Radke, July 2026.',
    stats: [
      ['Founded','May 2008 by Eva Radke (Fort Greene, then Long Island City, then Gowanus)'],
      ['Location','11,000 sq ft warehouse, Gowanus, Brooklyn (from ~2010)'],
      ['Type','501(c)(3) nonprofit prop house and creative reuse center'],
      ['Material diverted','600+ tons over its lifetime (closure statement, 2015)'],
      ['Peak intake','~10 tons per month'],
      ['To charities','~60% of all intake redistributed'],
      ['EPA award','2014 U.S. EPA Region 2 Environmental Quality Award'],
      ['The Brooklyn chapter closed','June 2015. Cause: inability to secure the ~$100K/year that the retail floor did not cover. The work did not end; it relocated and continued.'],
      ['Founder\'s next','<a href="https://www.artcubenation.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ArtCube Nation</a>, the worldwide art-department network Radke grew from a 2007 Google group. Still founder and CEO; approaching its 20-year anniversary.'],
      ['Brand continues','Savannah, GA, as a separate 501(c)(3) under Samita Wolfe, a former Navy sailor Radke mentored, who reopened the doors on Earth Day 2017.']
    ],
    body: [
      `Eva Radke spent fifteen years in production, the last ten in the art department, working as a freelance coordinator on New York commercials, television, and film. She had grown up in a conservative Texas oil family and left for New York at twenty-two, funding her film books by selling her business textbooks back to the University of Texas. The irony was not lost on her later: an oil heiress who built a warehouse to keep things out of the ground.`,
      `The breaking point came in pieces. It started as a matter of respect for the art itself: scenic artists, true painters, would make big, beautiful, genuine paintings for a shoot, and the moment the camera stopped they went in the dumpster. "Our art is not garbage," she said. Then came the absurdity that made the waste impossible to ignore. For a January 2007 toothpaste commercial, a client wanted fresh green peppermint plants; Radke found a Florida grower to hydroponically raise them, chartered an empty prop plane to fly them to JFK, sent in her birth certificate for the tarmac permit, and staged a heated truck in one-degree cold to keep them alive. Seven thousand dollars of logistics. On the day, the production looked at the seven skids of hallowed mint and used the $250 plastic backups instead. And then, on a wrap that same season, forced to throw out a mid-century credenza that nobody would take before the 4:55 cutoff at Silvercup, she hit the wall: "If I can't give away a mid-century credenza in New York City, something is very wrong. I know so many people who would love this right now." Newly a mother, pumping milk in the cube truck to send home with the PAs, she decided her time was worth more than trashing the planet her son would inherit. She took a thirty-day cash job she tried to price herself out of, got it anyway, and used the stack to walk away from the business and start a nonprofit.`,
      `What she built was eleven thousand square feet of counter-argument, though it did not start that big. Film Biz Recycling opened in 2008 in a Fort Greene apartment, moved to a small Long Island City space, and only around 2010 reached the Gowanus warehouse it became known for. The model was simple and unglamorous: a one-stop drop-off for the paint, furniture, wardrobe, set dressing, and raw materials that productions sent to the dump, and a retail floor that worked like the most thoughtfully curated thrift store in the borough, because everything in it had come from a film set. Workshops, green-production seminars, a staff of about a dozen. Roughly sixty percent of everything through the loading dock found its way to local charities, arts programs, and reuse centers, and only two or three percent ever reached a landfill. At peak, ten tons arrived every month.`,
      `In May 2014, six years in, Film Biz Recycling held the first Golden Dumpster Awards at the warehouse. The Founder's Award for an Individual Displaying Utter Greatness went to Emellie O'Brien of Earth Angel. Radke had opened one of the first operations of its kind on the East Coast, and she was now publicly honoring the person who would carry the category forward. That same spring, the U.S. EPA gave Film Biz Recycling its 2014 Region 2 Environmental Quality Award.`,
      `The closure of the Brooklyn warehouse, when it came, was about money and not relevance. The prop floor covered roughly eighty percent of the budget, but the remaining twenty, about a hundred thousand dollars a year, was nowhere to be found, and a state development grant that had carried the gap for a while ran out. Film Biz Recycling did not fit the traditional charity funding model: it looked like a thrift store and acted like a public utility, and the institutions that might have funded it did not know which one they were looking at. The MPAA, in Radke's account, told her they already had a carting company. They thought she was a garbage operation groveling for a contract. The warehouse was vacated by late June 2015.`,
      `But "closed" is the wrong verb for what happened next, and Radke is quick to correct it: "It moved. It didn't go out of business." The Film Biz Recycling name reopened in Savannah under Samita Wolfe and still does the work it was built for. And the deeper engine had already outgrown the warehouse. Out of a 2007 Google group named for the ArtCube trucks that fetch whatever a set needs, Radke had built ArtCube Nation, now a worldwide professional network for art-department, fabrication, and design crews across more than a hundred cities, a peer-to-peer marketplace that does online, without rent, what the warehouse did on the ground. Its most remarkable hour came during the early pandemic, when ArtCube's makers, prop fabricators, and seamstresses became an ad-hoc PPE supply chain for New York City hospitals, hand-delivering face shields and gear across all five boroughs while the official supply sat stalled at quota. A pulmonologist later estimated to Radke that the stopgap may have prevented on the order of a hundred thousand COVID exposures in the window before the real supply chain caught up. "I will die for this community," she says, "because this is who these people are." Art department, in her telling, is simply the part of any world that steps up and makes something out of nothing, fast, with no room for error, and it is exactly that instinct, pointed at waste instead of a shot list, that she has spent her life organizing.`,
      `Radke was always clear that the loss of the warehouse was the field's and not only hers. "It's like watching a unicorn die," she said of the closing. But she also said the thing that turned out to be true: that Obi-Wan and Dumbledore were stronger in death, and that maybe her impact would be felt more once the thing itself was gone. She was right. The people who came after learned from what she built and from what it cost her, and she is still here, still building, twenty years in, the lightning point that lit a great deal of what followed.`
    ],
    quote: [
      { text: `"Obi-Wan and Dumbledore were stronger in death, so maybe my impact will be felt even more now."`, attr: 'Eva Radke, founder, Film Biz Recycling, on its closing, Brokelyn, April 2015', color: 'rust' },
      { text: `"It moved. It didn't go out of business."`, attr: 'Eva Radke, correcting the record, 2026', color: 'rust' }
    ],
    links: [
      { label: 'Find Eva on LinkedIn', url: 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/eva-radke-45a1777/' },
      { label: 'See what Eva is up to now with ArtCube', url: 'https://artcubenation.com/blog/' }
    ],
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-extract', label: 'Why this matters', text: `Eva Radke built one of the first creative-reuse centers of its kind on the East Coast and ran it for seven years, turning what film sets threw away into a warehouse the whole borough could shop for free. The work was excellent, and the model genuinely new. What is easy to get wrong is the ending: the Brooklyn warehouse closed, but the work did not stop and the mission did not fail. The name still operates today in Savannah, Georgia, and Radke herself is still at it, approaching twenty years, running a worldwide art-department network she grew alongside it. As she puts it, she was "the lightning point," the most visible spark in a movement that had been waiting for someone loud enough to make people see it. Nearly everyone doing this work today is downstream of that spark.` }
  },
  'skater-girl': {
    bCls: 'badge-regen', bLabel: 'Regenerative',
    meta: 'Rajasthan, India · Independent, 2019 · Acquired by Netflix, 2021 · Dir. Manjari Makijany · Prod. Vinati Makijany, Emmanuel Pappas',
    name: 'Skater Girl',
    editorial: 'The Gold Standard',
    provenance: 'Built from a conversation with Vinati Makijany, August 2026.',
    stats: [
      ['What was built','Desert Dolphin Skatepark &mdash; 14,500 sq ft, reinforced concrete, Khempur, Rajasthan'],
      ['Scale','One of India\'s largest; the first that part of Rajasthan had ever seen (per filmmakers)'],
      ['Built in','45 days during monsoon season, 2018, by 100 Ramps (Darius Bharucha) with international volunteers'],
      ['Park opened','October 2018, before filming began (wrap spring 2019)'],
      ['Financing','Independent &mdash; private equity. Netflix acquired the finished film (streaming June 2021)'],
      ['Local hiring','More than 350 local people employed on the production'],
      ['Children impacted','Hundreds trained, including 35 girls (2021); foundation reports thousands since 2018'],
      ['National championship','Four Khempur skaters carried Rajasthan, April 2021'],
      ['Stewarded by','Living Grace Foundation (Vinati Makijany, Founder &amp; Managing Trustee)'],
      ['Production company','Skatepark Films, in association with Mac Productions.'],
      ['Still active','Eight years on the ground; a documentary in the cut; a foundation now opening to support']
    ],
    body: [
      `<strong>The correction the record needs</strong>`,
      `Almost everything written about Skater Girl calls it a Netflix film. It was not. The sisters made it independently, financing it themselves and through a private investor, and Netflix acquired the finished film only after seeing it complete. "We made it independently. Netflix came on board when they saw a finished film." The distinction matters more than a footnote, because it changes the meaning of what they did. This was not a streaming giant underwriting a philanthropic gesture out of a marketing budget. It was two sisters and their family making this happen and spending their own money , "every penny that you have has to be spent so mindfully" and choosing, at real personal cost, to pour a permanent skatepark into a village that had never seen one. The generosity was not corporate. It was theirs.`,
      `<strong>How it began</strong>`,
      `It began, as the best of these stories do, with a refusal to separate the art from the care.`,
      `The Makijany sisters grew up in Bombay in a bungalow across from a Catholic church, daughters of the celebrated Bollywood actor Mac Mohan, a man who had appeared in over two hundred films and who rehearsed Shakespeare in the mornings, treating his body, as Vinati remembers it, "as an instrument for his art." Their mother is a nurse. "So the art and the care aspect of it became something that we saw in our home," Vinati says, the two halves of who she and Manjari would become, present in the house before either of them chose a thing. As children they crossed the road to help a woman teaching street kids at the church, not out of virtue but because, as Vinati puts it, "we have free time, this is what we should be doing."`,
      `Skater Girl was their first feature, Manjari directing, Vinati producing, both sisters co-writing with Manjari's husband and co-producer, Emmanuel Pappas. And when they were writing a story about a girl who discovers skateboarding, Manjari said the thing that would define everything after: "We're talking about building a skate park, we should just build a skate park." Emmanuel agreed on the spot. There was no strategy in it, no plan to be change-makers. "We didn't think this would be a blueprint," Vinati says. "We were just like, okay, yeah, we're doing this."`,
      `The instinct was to keep the giving and the making in the same place. Someone offered them land elsewhere in Rajasthan on the condition that others would manage the park after the shoot, and they said no. "We want the place to be a free community space," Vinati says. "We don't want anyone sabotaging that approach." In retrospect, she knows how it looked: "Anyone would consider that a stupid idea, why are you taking on a responsibility for life?" They took it on anyway.`,
      `<strong>Why Rajasthan, and how they chose</strong>`,
      `They chose the state on purpose. Rajasthan ranked highest on India's Patriarchy Index, a fact the sisters knew before there was a tidy name for it, because, as Vinati says, "we've spent time in India, we recognize the states where women are treated like second-class citizens." It made cinematic sense and it made sense for impact.`,
      `But choosing a state is not choosing a village, and the sisters spent fifteen days driving across Rajasthan carrying two agendas no ordinary location scout carries: a place to shoot, and land to build on. They traveled with a skateboard, watching how children responded to it. And when they reached the village that felt right, they saw something that settled it: the children there had already made their own skateboards, one little boy dragging a plank on ball bearings across the dirt. That improvised board is in the finished film. "When we saw that," Vinati says, "we just felt: this is where the film needs to be set."`,
      `<strong>The build</strong>`,
      `The build was real construction, and it was hard. The production brought in 100 Ramps, the Bengaluru firm led by pioneering Indian skater Darius Bharucha assembled a crew of stoked volunteers from across the world, and poured 14,500 square feet of reinforced concrete over 45 days, in the monsoon. It was one of the largest skateparks in India and the first that part of Rajasthan had ever seen. Since then, the skateboarding scene has picked up and now the country boasts of many more skateparks.`,
      `The villagers did not know what they were watching. In a desert state, some thought the sisters were building a swimming pool ("where are you going to find the water?"). Someone guessed a parking lot. Someone guessed a mall. Even one of the volunteers building it accused the sisters of only caring about their film, and Vinati remembers the sting of it, wanting to tell him he had no idea what their intentions were. She does not blame him, she says: it was so rare for anyone to do this that he had no reference point for it.`,
      `A skate coach was brought in months ahead of the shoot to train not only the actors but the local children, so that by the time filming began the village kids were skating fearlessly, and most of the young skaters in the film are those children, not imported extras. The production hired more than 350 local people. For the climax, the sisters brought in real skateboarders from across India, because, as Vinati says, "we're making a film about skateboarding and it cannot be done without them."`,
      `<strong>What it asked of her</strong>`,
      `Here is what the research could never show, and what the film's tidy success story leaves out: the years after.`,
      `The park opened in October 2018. The film wrapped in the spring of 2019 and went up on Netflix in June 2021. But for Vinati, that was not an ending, it was the start of an eight-year commitment that is still going. She set up the Living Grace Foundation so that every donation would move through a clean channel. And then she stayed, and met the full weight of what building something in that village actually meant.`,
      `She has watched a mother walk into the park with a brick, threatening her own son to make him leave. She has stood in front of a shepherd-community girl who was threatened with being burned alive for the crime of being invited to skate. A farmer once tried to hand her his daughter to raise, "just take my daughter, you'll be able to raise her well" because he could not feed three girls, and she had to find the strength to say no without letting it break her. For years the local men would not meet her eye or speak to her; some addressed her as a man, unused to reporting to a woman. A government official looked her in the face and demanded to know her "selfish agenda" - "why else would you do it?" Two years later, another official told her what she was doing was amazing.`,
      `It cost money, too, the kind of cost that does not show up in a budget line. "We've personally broken our savings, our mutual funds, stocks," Vinati says, "and continue to put in this." They are only now, cautiously, beginning to open the work to outside support.`,
      `And it changed her. To carry it, she went to INSEAD to study change management, "to even deal with what you do when you have too much empathy." She is now working with a sociologist and a psychologist on a research paper about what a skatepark actually shifts in a village: whether sport can, in her words, "inculcate curiosity, courage, and care." Because that, in the end, is the point, not medals. "We're not trying to train champions. We're not an academy." That some of the Khempur children went on to represent the state is wonderful, but it is not the measure. The measure is whether a child can begin to imagine a different life, the way the girl the sisters met behind a thorn-bush barricade, forbidden to leave her home, had never once been asked what she wanted to do. "No one's ever asked me that question," she told them. That line is in the film.`,
      `<strong>What is still true</strong>`,
      `The park never closed. Skateboards and safety gear are still free. The Living Grace Foundation and the Skate Basti Foundation still keep the lights on. And in April 2021, four of its young skaters, the youngest around ten, most of them leaving their village for the first time in their lives carried Rajasthan's colors to the national championship in Mohali.`,
      `The sisters are still filming there, too. They have been documenting the village for eight years and are cutting a documentary from it. And they are already doing it again: their next film will be adopting a similar model in southern India, impact designed in from the first day, the same principle, and once again sport is a theme.`,
      `Vinati never set out to found a tradition. When told she belonged in a lineage running back a century, from Mary Pickford's relief bucket in 1921 to her own slab in the desert, she recognized it at once. She had simply lived it before anyone gave it a name.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"We wanted to make clear that we're not coming here just to make a movie and go away. We were creating something that was going to live beyond the film."`, attr: `Vinati Makijany, Producer, Skater Girl, Women's Media Center, June 2021`, color: 'moss' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-regen', label: 'Our Take', text: `Skater Girl did not give back what was left over. Vinati and Manjari Makijany built the community asset first, then made the film around it, and eight years on, they are still there. The cleanest evidence the Ledger has found that community benefit was designed in, not added on, is not a line in a press kit. It is the order of operations: the skatepark opened in October 2018, and the cameras did not roll until the spring of 2019. By the time the film was shot, the children of the village had already been skating their own park for months. It was theirs before it was ever a location. What makes it the gold standard of the Ledger is not only that it was permanent. It is that the sisters treated the giving as inseparable from the filmmaking, "we want the filmmaking and the impact part of it to go hand in hand," as Vinati puts it, and then stayed to carry the weight of what they had started, long after every other production would have struck the set and gone home.` }
  },
  'sing-sing': {
    bCls: 'badge-regen', bLabel: 'Regenerative',
    meta: 'A24 · 2024 · Dir. Greg Kwedar',
    name: 'Sing Sing',
    editorial: 'The Equity Model',
    stats: [
      ['Director','Greg Kwedar'],
      ['Screenplay','Kwedar & Clint Bentley; story also by Clarence Maclin & John Whitfield'],
      ['Lead actor','Colman Domingo'],
      ['Pay structure','Same SAG scale rate for all cast and crew, star included'],
      ['Equity structure','Pro-rata profit participation for entire cast and crew (80+ people)'],
      ['The waterfall','60/40 cast-crew split from first dollar; 50/50 after investors recoup 120%'],
      ['Cast','Formerly incarcerated alumni of Rehabilitation Through the Arts (RTA), as themselves'],
      ['Prison release','First film released to theaters and prisons at once (A24/RTA/Edovo, Jan 17, 2025)']
    ],
    body: [
      `What makes Sing Sing belong in the Regenerative category of the Ledger is not why Greg Kwedar and Clint Bentley made the film. The question is why they structured it the way they did. The film, which premiered at Toronto in 2023 and reached theaters through A24 in 2024, is built around the real Rehabilitation Through the Arts program at Sing Sing Correctional Facility, and its cast is almost entirely formerly incarcerated alumni of that program, playing versions of themselves alongside Colman Domingo in the lead.`,
      `Years of development produced a film with a specific, unusual deal structure. Everyone on set, from the lead actor to the production assistants, was paid the same SAG scale day rate. And everyone, cast and crew, received a pro-rata share of the producers' equity. Not a charitable gesture, not a nominal points deal, but a real ownership pool: more than eighty people with a stake in the outcome. The mechanics were deliberate. Bentley has described a waterfall in which cast and crew share from the first dollar, and once investors recoup their capital and then some, the split moves to fifty-fifty in perpetuity. Ownership, structured to last beyond the box office.`,
      `The structure only held because the top of the call sheet agreed to it. Domingo, an Academy Award nominee, took the same rate as a first-day production assistant. Kwedar was blunt about why that mattered: the model does not function unless the number one on the call sheet buys in. He did. And the deeper point was never only about money. Clarence Maclin and John Whitfield, two of the formerly incarcerated men the film is about, share its story credit, and when Sing Sing was nominated for Best Adapted Screenplay, they became the first RTA alumni ever nominated for an Academy Award. The men were not subjects of the story. They were authors of it, and owners of it.`,
      `Kwedar said as much to Variety at the Brooklyn Academy of Music premiere in June 2024: in a story built around people who had had ownership stripped from them, the whole cast and crew would have literal ownership of their own story. The commitment extended past the deal memo. On January 17, 2025, A24 and Rehabilitation Through the Arts, working with the prison-education platform Edovo, made Sing Sing the first film released to theaters and to prisons at the same time, so that men currently incarcerated could see the work that men formerly incarcerated had made.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"We have over 80 profit participants in our half of the business. That's our whole cast and crew. In a story like this, which centers formerly incarcerated men who have been taken advantage of by systems, they have literal ownership over their own story."`, attr: 'Greg Kwedar, Director, Sing Sing, Variety, June 30, 2024', color: 'moss' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-regen', label: 'Our Take', text: `Skater Girl built permanent physical infrastructure for the community its story was about. Sing Sing built permanent equity infrastructure for the people its story was about. Both asked the same question in different registers. Skater Girl: what does this community need that we could also use? Sing Sing: what do these men need, after a lifetime of being taken from, that we could also use? The answer in both cases was ownership. Not gratitude. Not charity. Ownership, designed in from day one. In a film about men whom every system had stripped of agency, the cast did not merely appear in the story. They were paid as equals in it, held equity in it, and in two cases were nominated for an Academy Award for writing it.` }
  },
  'mptf': {
    bCls: 'badge-film', bLabel: 'Filmanthropic',
    meta: 'Founded 1921',
    name: 'Motion Picture Relief Fund',
    editorial: 'The Century of Care',
    stats: [
      ['Founded','1921'],
      ['Prime mover','Mary Pickford (age 29), founding vice-president'],
      ['First president','Joseph M. Schenck; Rev. Neal Dodd, administrator'],
      ['1932 innovation','Payroll Pledge Program: 0.5% of wages over $200/week'],
      ['Screen Guild Theatre','1939-1952; star fees funded the campus ($5.2M raised)'],
      ['Campus','Woodland Hills (Wasserman Campus), land bought 1940, dedicated 1942'],
      ['Helped (2020)','Nearly 10,000 (MPTF CEO Bob Beitcher, Variety, 2022)'],
      ['Recognition','Jean Hersholt Humanitarian Award, 2021, the first ever given to an organization'],
      ['Renamed','Motion Picture &amp; Television Fund, 1971']
    ],
    body: [
      `The American film industry of 1921 was making a handful of its biggest names extraordinarily wealthy and leaving the rest of its workforce with almost nothing when things went wrong. A worker hurt on set had no compensation. A performer whose career ended had no pension. The industry had the money, the talent, and the audience. It had no mechanism to care for its own.`,
      `Mary Pickford decided that gap was hers to close. She was twenty-nine, and two years earlier she had co-founded United Artists with Chaplin, Fairbanks, and Griffith, on the conviction that the most powerful people in an industry could remake its economics if they chose to. The Motion Picture Relief Fund was that same conviction turned from the industry's ownership to its welfare. She drove it as its founding vice-president; Joseph Schenck served as its first president and the Reverend Neal Dodd, an Episcopal priest who played clergymen in hundreds of films, ran its day to day.`,
      `What Pickford built was not a donation. It was a mechanism. The Fund began with coin boxes set out on studio floors, money dropped by the people working that day for the people who weren't. As MPTF still tells it, Pickford kept a little galvanized bucket on her own sets, and if you were lucky enough to be working, she expected you to put something in. By 1932 she had formalized it: the Payroll Pledge Program, under which everyone earning more than two hundred dollars a week gave half of one percent of their wages, automatically. In a single month of 1933, the Fund bought groceries for more than two thousand five hundred film workers and kept seventy-five families from eviction.`,
      `In 1939 the actor Jean Hersholt, by then the Fund's president, helped launch the Screen Guild Theatre, a weekly radio program on which the biggest names in Hollywood performed and donated their fees. It ran until 1952 and raised more than five million dollars, and that money bought land. In Woodland Hills, on what had been forty-odd acres of walnut and orange groves, the Fund broke ground in 1941 and dedicated the Motion Picture Country House in 1942: a place for the industry's elders and most vulnerable to grow old among their own. It operates still, today the Wasserman Campus, under the name the Fund took in 1971, the Motion Picture &amp; Television Fund. Per Variety's centennial reporting, nearly ten thousand people received its help in 2020 alone.`,
      `There is a circle worth closing here. The Academy's award for humanitarian work is named for Jean Hersholt, the man who ran this Fund for eighteen years and raised the money that built its campus. In 2021, for the first time in its history, the Academy gave that award not to a person but to an organization: to the Motion Picture Relief Fund itself. The honor named for the man came home to the institution he had built. A hundred years after a twenty-nine-year-old passed a bucket on a silent-film set, the industry's own highest body of recognition bent its rules to say that the care had been worth as much as the art.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"Actors are only ever between engagements."`, attr: "Mary Pickford's reported insistence that the Woodland Hills home be called a House, not a Home (MPTF history)", color: 'gold' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-film', label: 'Our Take', text: `In 1921 the question was not what the industry should do for itself. It was what one of its most powerful people could build with the leverage she had while she had it. Mary Pickford answered with infrastructure that has now lasted a century: a working campus that still takes people in, a payroll pledge that made giving a normal line on the check, and, in 2021, an Academy Award given for the first time not to a person but to the institution she founded. The Motion Picture Relief Fund is proof that philanthropy designed in early enough, and backed by enough power to stick, can outlast everyone who built it.` }
  },
  'hobbiton': {
    bCls: 'badge-sust', bLabel: 'Sustainable / Adjacent',
    meta: 'New Line Cinema · 2001–2003 / The Hobbit · Warner Bros. · 2012–2014',
    name: 'The Lord of the Rings',
    editorial: 'The Set That Became a Country',
    stats: [
      ['Site','Alexander family farm, Matamata, Waikato, NZ (approx. 1,250 acres)'],
      ['Set area','Approx. 12 acres'],
      ['Site discovered','Aerial location search, 1998'],
      ['Original Hobbiton','37-39 temporary hobbit holes (1999); mostly cleared after filming'],
      ['Facades left','~17 bare plywood facades survived (rain halted demolition)'],
      ['Tours began','2002 (Russell Alexander, son of original owner Ian)'],
      ['Permanent rebuild','2010-2011 for The Hobbit; 44 permanent hobbit holes today'],
      ['Ownership today','Alexander family and Peter Jackson, roughly 50/50'],
      ['Visitors in 2019','650,000 (pre-Covid peak)'],
      ['Tourism value','~$630M NZD/yr film-influenced visitor spend (Tourism NZ, 2019)']
    ],
    body: [
      `When the Lord of the Rings crew finished filming the Shire and moved on, they left behind, on the Alexander family's working sheep and beef farm in Matamata, the bones of Hobbiton. The set had been built of plywood and polystyrene, meant to be temporary. An attempt to clear it was halted by heavy rain, and roughly seventeen bare hobbit-hole facades were left standing in the paddock, with the understanding that the crew would return to finish the job. The film was done. The lease was satisfied. The Alexanders were left with seventeen movie-set remnants and a decision.`,
      `They kept them. Russell Alexander, a son of the farmer the location scouts had first approached in 1998, opened tours in 2002 and spent years turning curious foot traffic into a working tourism business, through the family's own initiative. The production that had found the farm by air, sent the New Zealand Army to build a 1.5-kilometer access road, and raised the most beloved set in modern cinema on a sheep paddock had left with no obligation to the family's economic future. The family engineered that future themselves.`,
      `Then the value the family had proven drew the production back. When Peter Jackson returned in 2009 to prepare The Hobbit, Hobbiton was rebuilt, this time in stone and timber, meant to last. Construction across 2010 and 2011 produced the forty-four permanent hobbit holes and the Green Dragon Inn that visitors see today. And the deal had changed: Jackson became a fifty-percent partner in the tourism operation. The set that a production had once walked away from was now one the filmmaker co-owned. By 2019, Hobbiton drew 650,000 visitors, and Tourism New Zealand estimated that film-influenced travel was worth around 630 million New Zealand dollars a year to the country.`,
      `There is a shadow on the case, and it has been named on the record. Scholars including Misha Kavka, Stephen Turner, and Alice Te Punga Somerville have argued that the Tolkien-tourism wave rebranded the New Zealand landscape as Middle-earth and, in doing so, overwrote the land's own history, including its precolonial Maori history, in the story sold to visitors. The critique is documented in the academic literature. It is not fringe, and it is not the Ledger's place to adjudicate it. The reader who wants to follow the thread can. The Ledger owes the reader the sentence.`
    ],
    quote: null,
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-sust', label: 'Our Take', text: `Name the good first: a working sheep farm became one of the most beloved destinations in the Southern Hemisphere, raised from the plywood bones a film crew left in a paddock, because one family decided the leftover scenery was worth keeping. It now employs hundreds and draws visitors from every continent. Hobbiton is the largest residual community asset in modern film history, and it became one almost by accident, so completely that once the family had proven the value, the production came back to buy in, with Peter Jackson taking a fifty-percent stake in the business itself. None of this is a failure. It is a measure of how much was always sitting in the land, and of how much further it might have gone had anyone seen it there at the start, the way Skater Girl did, rather than twenty years later.` }
  },
  'field-of-dreams': {
    bCls: 'badge-sust', bLabel: 'Sustainable / Adjacent',
    meta: 'Universal Pictures · 1989 · Dir. Phil Alden Robinson · Dyersville, Iowa',
    name: 'Field of Dreams',
    editorial: 'The Accidental Asset',
    stats: [
      ['Production','Field of Dreams, Universal Pictures, 1989'],
      ['Director','Phil Alden Robinson'],
      ['Location','Dyersville, Iowa (Lansing and Ameskamp family farms)'],
      ['What was left','A baseball diamond, kept at wrap with no community-benefit agreement'],
      ['Design intent','None. The benefit was not designed in.'],
      ['Family-run era','~1989-2011, free public access, owner-run'],
      ['2021-2022','MLB \'Field of Dreams\' games (White Sox/Yankees 2021; Cubs/Reds 2022)'],
      ['2022 plan','$80M private expansion announced under Frank Thomas (later refocused)'],
      ['2024-2026','Site bought by a local nonprofit ($27M); ~$120M civic campaign underway'],
      ['May 2026','$13M committed: $10M from three Iowa families/firms + $3M state (Telegraph Herald)']
    ],
    body: [
      `Universal Pictures built a baseball diamond in the middle of a cornfield outside Dyersville, Iowa, in 1988, because Phil Alden Robinson's film needed one. The diamond straddled two farms: the infield and right field on Don Lansing's land, the rest of the outfield on the neighboring Ameskamp property. When filming ended, the production left the field behind. Not as a planned gift, not as a contractual obligation, simply because it was there and the studio had no reason to remove it. The families owned the land. The diamond sat on it. They had to decide what to do.`,
      `What they did, over the next three decades, is the entire argument for this entry. They kept it. They opened it to visitors, free, and for years the two families ran competing little attractions a few feet apart, each with its own gift shop, before the land was finally united under one owner in 2007. They managed a slow swell of baseball pilgrims with no production support and no blueprint for turning a leftover set into something lasting. Don Lansing simply left bats and balls and gloves out for whoever came. By the film's twentieth anniversary the site drew around 65,000 people a year, on word of mouth alone.`,
      `Then the value the families had proven began drawing everyone else in. The property sold in 2011, and in 2021 a group led by the Hall of Famer Frank Thomas took control and, in April 2022, announced an eighty-million-dollar private plan for youth fields, dormitories, and a hotel. That plan stalled and was scaled back, and in 2024 the site passed to a local nonprofit for twenty-seven million dollars, much of it state money, now pursuing a roughly hundred-and-twenty-million-dollar campaign to build a permanent ballpark and a year-round youth complex. Major League Baseball had already come, playing regular-season games beside the cornfield in 2021 and 2022, with another set for 2026. In May 2026, three Iowa families and businesses pledged ten million dollars and the state of Iowa added three more.`,
      `One detail closes the circle better than any figure. The youth fields now rising next to the movie diamond are being lit, in part, by Musco Lighting, the Iowa company that lit the original film set in 1988. The same hands that lit it for the cameras are lighting it for the children. The benefit arrived, real and large and still growing. It simply took thirty-five years and a great many people other than the studio to build what the studio left in a field.`
    ],
    quote: null,
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-sust', label: 'Our Take', text: `Begin with the thing itself, because it is genuinely lovely. A studio built a baseball diamond in a cornfield and went home, and two Iowa farm families decided to keep it, mowed it, opened it, and let anyone who needed to walk into the corn do exactly that, free, for thirty years. It became one of the few places in American life where grown men weep in public and no one thinks it strange. The Ledger honors that without reservation, and lingers only on the timing. What the families proved, and others later arrived to build on, a single line in a production plan could have begun on day one, the way Skater Girl did. The benefit was always sitting in the cornfield, waiting to be designed for.` }
  },
  'wicked': {
    bCls: 'badge-sust', bLabel: 'Sustainable / Adjacent',
    meta: 'Universal Pictures · 2024 · Dir. Jon M. Chu',
    name: 'Wicked',
    editorial: 'The Ecological Choice',
    stats: [
      ['Designer','Nathan Crowley (won the 2025 Oscar for this work)'],
      ['Farm','The Eves family nursery (Belmont Nurseries / Norfolk Tulips), Norfolk, England'],
      ['Owners','Mark and Susanne Eves'],
      ['Tulips planted','9 million (Crowley asked for 1 million; the farmer said 9)'],
      ['Field size','Around 20-25 acres'],
      ['Used as','Cut heads used as decorative roof material on Munchkin homes (per design press)'],
      ['Cost','~0.25% of the $145M budget; comparable to the VFX alternative'],
      ['Post-wrap','Bulbs replanted; fields reopened for The Norfolk Hospice Tapping House'],
      ['Charity result','‘Tulips for Tapping’ (begun 2021); over GBP 1M raised cumulatively']
    ],
    body: [
      `For Wicked, production designer Nathan Crowley insisted on real tulips. Not CGI, not silk, not painted backdrops. He told Variety he knew it could work because he had already grown five hundred acres of corn for Christopher Nolan's Interstellar a decade earlier. The principle was the same: real beats manufactured when a team is willing to do the work, and find the right farmer. His reasons, on the record, were craft and vision, not charity. What the choice flowed into afterward was something he could not have planned.`,
      `How many tulips became a small accident of its own. Crowley has said he asked for a million. Mark and Susanne Eves, the Norfolk growers he found, told him the number he actually wanted was nine million, and nine million is what arrived: reds, pinks, and whites planted across some twenty-odd acres to become Munchkinland's fields. The image that defines the film was partly a misunderstanding, grown real. And it was not the extravagance it looks like. At roughly four cents a bulb, nine million tulips came to about a quarter of one percent of the hundred-and-forty-five-million-dollar budget, on the order of what rendering them would have cost. The cut heads, once filming was done, were used as decorative roofing on the Munchkin houses.`,
      `Then the flowers did something the production never scripted. The Eves family replanted the bulbs and reopened the fields to the public, and the proceeds went to The Norfolk Hospice Tapping House, which provides free end-of-life care across West Norfolk. The openings, 'Tulips for Tapping,' had begun back in 2021, three years before Wicked, and the film's fame poured into them: record crowds, and across its run more than a million pounds for the hospice, roughly two months of everything it costs to keep the place running. A commercial flower farm and a charity it already loved were lifted by a film that simply chose to shoot among real things. None of that was designed. All of it was good. That is precisely why it is worth noticing.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"I knew it could work because I grew 500 acres of corn on Interstellar. I knew I could, with the right farmer."`, attr: 'Nathan Crowley, Production Designer, Wicked, Variety', color: 'sage' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-sust', label: 'Our Take', text: `Start with what is simply good: nine million tulips, grown for a film, went on to raise more than a million pounds for a hospice that cares for the dying. The Ledger says that plainly, and it also notices how little it cost. Doing it for real ran to roughly what rendering it digitally would have. The ecological choice was not the expensive, sacrificial one; it was the equal one, and it still ended in mercy. That is exactly why it is worth looking past such a lovely outcome: the good here arrived by happy accident, downstream of a farm that already gave. Imagine the same instinct aimed on purpose, nine million tulips chosen on day one for the garden of a hospital, a school, a food desert. Same budget, by design. The flowers proved the principle; the next production gets to author it.` }
  },
  'interstellar': {
    bCls: 'badge-whatif', bLabel: 'What If',
    meta: 'Warner Bros. / Paramount · 2014 · Dir. Christopher Nolan',
    name: 'Interstellar',
    editorial: 'The Harvest That Fed No One',
    stats: [
      ['Location','500 acres near Longview, Alberta, ~35 mi south of Calgary'],
      ['Designer','Nathan Crowley'],
      ['Film budget','Approx. $165 million'],
      ['Worldwide gross','~$773 million (with re-releases)'],
      ['Harvest fate','Sold at a profit that offset production costs'],
      ['People fed','Zero'],
      ['Nearby need (2013)','Calgary Food Bank: $43M of food, 136,000 client visits, demand doubled since 2008'],
      ['Also nearby','~60% of Alberta First Nations households food insecure (Treaty 7 land)']
    ],
    body: [
      `Christopher Nolan's conviction on Interstellar was that the farmland had to be real. Five hundred acres of corn, planted near Longview, Alberta, on rolling land beneath the Rockies where corn is not usually grown, which was the point: the film needed a crop struggling where it did not belong. Nathan Crowley, who would later grow nine million tulips for Wicked, was the production designer. He knew the tulips were possible partly because Interstellar had already proved that real agriculture at film scale could be done when a team committed to it.`,
      `The corn grew, through a cold snap and the catastrophic Alberta floods of June 2013. At a Hollywood Reporter directors' roundtable, Nolan described its fate plainly: a good crop, sold at a profit that helped offset the budget. The film went on to gross around three-quarters of a billion dollars.`,
      `Hold that against what the film is about. Interstellar imagines a near future in which blight has killed humanity's crops one by one until corn is nearly the last thing that will still grow, and the survival of the species depends on whether the next generation can be fed. To tell that story, the production grew five hundred acres of food, real food, on real Alberta soil, and harvested it in a year when the Calgary Food Bank an hour up the road was distributing forty-three million dollars of food across a hundred and thirty-six thousand client visits, its demand doubled since 2008 and a flood deepening the need. The land they grew it on is Treaty 7 territory, where roughly sixty percent of First Nations households were food insecure. A film about feeding a starving world grew a field of grain beside hungry people and sent it to market.`,
      `The industry already had the means. Groups like Rock and Wrap It Up, EcoSet, and Earth Angel have moved millions of meals and tons of material out of productions and into communities for years. What a film like this one could use is someone in pre-production whose job is to spot the opening, design the give-back in, and put it before the director and producers early enough to act on. With a crop, that window is the planting, not the wrap. When the role exists, a harvest like this feeds someone. When it does not, the default takes over, and a field of food becomes a line item.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"In the end, we got a pretty good crop, and we actually made money on this."`, attr: 'Christopher Nolan, on the Interstellar corn harvest, The Hollywood Reporter', color: 'rust' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-extract', label: 'Our Take', text: `Nolan had exactly the creative conviction Filmanthropy is built on: real over digital, five hundred living acres rather than a green screen. The instinct could not have been more right, and it stopped one question short of something extraordinary. Who could this harvest feed? That the question went unanswered is not a knock on Nolan, who is owed his due, and not a charge of greed. It is the whole argument of this Ledger in a single field: when giving is not designed into a production, even a film about feeding a starving world will grow real food beside real hunger and send it to market.` }
  },
  'patch-adams': {
    bCls: 'badge-whatif', bLabel: 'What If',
    meta: 'Universal Pictures · 1998 · Dir. Tom Shadyac',
    name: 'Patch Adams',
    editorial: 'The Broken Promise',
    stats: [
      ['Global gross','$202.3 million worldwide'],
      ['Williams salary',"Reportedly $21M (Adams's claim; trade estimates nearer $10M, unconfirmed)"],
      ['Adams received','Fees that built three structures: a farmhouse, workshop, and dacha (per Gesundheit!)'],
      ["Universal's closing card",'Per the Institute, stated the hospital was built; it was not'],
      ['Hospital planned','44-bed free care facility, Pocahontas County, West Virginia'],
      ['Hospital status','Still unbuilt as of 2026 (sought since 1971)'],
      ['The film itself','Commercial hit, critically panned (RT 21%); Adams felt reduced to a \'funny doctor\'']
    ],
    body: [
      `When Universal Pictures approached Hunter 'Patch' Adams about a film based on his life, he understood it, in his own telling, as the thing that would finally build his hospital. He had been working toward a free care facility in rural West Virginia since 1971, the central purpose of the Gesundheit Institute he founded. The film, from Adams's perspective, was the chance to fund the work of his life.`,
      `The film was made, and it was a phenomenon: a two-hundred-and-two-million-dollar worldwide gross, Robin Williams in the title role. What came back to the Institute, per its own published history, were fees enough to build three structures on its land, a farmhouse, a workshop, and a dacha that still host its courses and workshops. Real buildings, real and ongoing programs. Not a hospital. And, by the Institute's account, Universal closed the film with a card stating the hospital had been built, which it had not. Donors who saw it assumed the work was done, and the fundraising grew harder in the years that followed.`,
      `Adams has never hidden what the film cost him beyond the money. He told Roger Ebert, who recounted it, 'I hate that movie,' and explained elsewhere that it flattened a life of systemic critique into something smaller: 'I would become a funny doctor. Imagine how shallow that is relative to who I am.' His sharpest words have been about the economics, repeated publicly for years: that Williams was paid a fortune to play him and not a dollar of it reached the free hospital. It is worth being careful here, because the figure Adams cites has never been confirmed by any studio or trade outlet, and because the implication, taken alone, would be deeply unfair to the man who played him.`,
      `Robin Williams was, by nearly every account, extraordinarily generous. He supported dozens of causes, gave a decade to St. Jude without a fee, raised tens of millions through Comic Relief, and entertained close to ninety thousand service members for the USO. No record shows a gift to Gesundheit specifically, and Adams's frustration on that narrow point is his to voice. But Adams himself has been just as clear about the larger truth. After Williams died in 2014, he wrote that he was 'enormously grateful for his wonderful performance of my early life, which has allowed the Gesundheit Institute to continue and expand our work.' The quarrel was never with the actor. It was with a process that built a film about a free hospital and never built the hospital. Now in his eighties, Adams is still working. The hospital is still unbuilt.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"Robin Williams made $21 million for four months of pretending to be me, in a very simplistic version, and did not give $10 to my free hospital. Patch Adams, the person, would have, if I had Robin's money, given all $21 million to a free hospital in a country where 80 million cannot get care."`, attr: `Dr. Hunter "Patch" Adams, in public statements (in print by 2009 and repeated since). The $21M figure is Adams's own and is not independently confirmed.`, color: 'rust' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-extract', label: 'Our Take', text: `The film did real good: its fees built three working structures for the Gesundheit Institute and helped launch a decade of its teaching and outreach. But the man at its center understood it as the thing that would finally build his free hospital, and twenty-eight years and two hundred million dollars later, that hospital is still unbuilt. The story had the subject, the star, and the audience. What it never had was anyone building the hospital into the production the way Skater Girl built its skatepark first. That is the broken promise, and it is the Ledger's clearest proof that a film can be about a thing and still fail to make the thing, when no one designs the giving in.` }
  },
  'black-panther': {
    bCls: 'badge-film', bLabel: 'Filmanthropic',
    meta: 'Marvel Studios / Walt Disney Studios · 2018 · Dir. Ryan Coogler',
    name: 'Black Panther',
    editorial: 'Wakanda in the Real World',
    stats: [
      ['Worldwide gross','$1.347 billion (2018); Wakanda Forever $859M (2022)'],
      ['2018 Disney gift','$1M to Boys &amp; Girls Clubs of America: 12 STEM Centers of Innovation'],
      ['The 12 cities','Atlanta, Baltimore, Chicago, Harlem, Hartford, Memphis, New Orleans, Oakland, Orlando, Philadelphia, Washington DC, Watts'],
      ['2022 Disney gift','$1M to Girls Who Code, Ghetto Film School, Hidden Genius Project, DonorsChoose + international'],
      ['Grassroots','#BlackPantherChallenge raised ~$1M, sent 75,000+ kids to the film free'],
      ['Cast scholarship','$250,000 full-ride scholarship presented by the cast (2018)'],
      ['Boseman','Visited St. Jude child cancer patients while privately battling cancer; gave $10K to the campaign'],
      ['Local economic impact','~$84M (2018) and ~$314M (2022) in Georgia wages and activity'],
      ['Oakland connection',"Coogler's hometown; T'Challa opens an outreach center there in the film"]
    ],
    body: [
      `What Black Panther gave back is usually told as a single number, and the single number undersells it. Begin instead with the range. Days into the film's run, while it was still the number one movie in the country and the cultural conversation was at full volume, The Walt Disney Company announced a million-dollar gift to the Boys and Girls Clubs of America to build twelve STEM Centers of Innovation. The timing was deliberate, made while the film's moment was alive rather than after the receipts were counted, and so was the design.`,
      `The twelve cities were not chosen at random. They map onto the communities of the film's most invested audience and the places where investment in young Black students of science was most overdue. One of them is Oakland, which carries the full weight of the film's intent. Oakland is Ryan Coogler's hometown, and Oakland is where T'Challa, in the film's closing scenes, opens an outreach center so that the technology of a fictional nation might lift real children. The donation and the story named the same city, on purpose. Four years later the studio did it again: a second million, for the sequel, to Girls Who Code, the Ghetto Film School, the Hidden Genius Project, and others at home and abroad.`,
      `But the studio's gift was only one current in a much wider flood, and not the largest. A young marketer named Frederick Joseph started a campaign to buy theater tickets for children who could not afford them; it spread to more than six hundred fundraisers and raised close to a million dollars, sending over seventy-five thousand kids to see themselves on the screen. The cast presented a quarter-million-dollar college scholarship. Chadwick Boseman, who was privately fighting the cancer that would take his life in 2020, spent time with young cancer patients at St. Jude and quietly added his own money to the ticket campaign. And the productions themselves poured roughly eighty-four million dollars into Georgia in 2018, and three hundred million more for the sequel, in wages and local work.`,
      `Set those side by side and a quiet truth appears: the fans who gave five dollars, and the actor who gave his hours while sick, reached deeper into what they had than the company with the most to give did. That is the question the whole Ledger asks, and Black Panther asks it more gently than most, because here the giving was genuine. When a studio decides early how far it will reach, and reaches as far as the people around it already do, a film like this one does not just inspire a generation of young scientists. It funds them at the scale of its own success.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"It is thrilling to see how inspired young audiences were by the spectacular technology in the film, so it's fitting that we show our appreciation by helping advance STEM programs for youth, especially in underserved areas of the country, to give them the knowledge and tools to build the future they want."`, attr: 'Bob Iger, Walt Disney Company, Disney press release, Feb 26, 2018', color: 'gold' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-film', label: 'Our Take', text: `Black Panther gave generously, and in more ways than the headlines caught. What stays with you, once all of it is counted, is a single quiet asymmetry: of everyone who reached toward the communities this film was made for, the studio that earned the most reached, proportionally, the least. That is not a charge of greed. The giving was real, early, and well aimed, which is exactly why the case belongs among the better ones. It is an invitation. A studio sets the ceiling on what a film can leave behind, and this one had room to set it higher.` }
  },
  'schindlers-list': {
    bCls: 'badge-film', bLabel: 'Filmanthropic',
    meta: 'Universal / Amblin · 1993 · Dir. Steven Spielberg',
    name: 'Schindler\'s List',
    editorial: 'Blood Money Into the World',
    stats: [
      ["Spielberg's personal profit","Zero, refused as 'blood money'"],
      ['Decided','Before filming, that no profit could go to him or his family'],
      ['Foundation founded','Righteous Persons Foundation, 1994, from his profit share'],
      ['Cumulative grants','$160M+ to date (RPF, 2026)'],
      ['Also founded 1994',"USC Shoah Foundation, now the world's largest survivor-testimony archive"],
      ['Recent','$30M endowment to the USC Shoah Foundation, late 2024'],
      ['Early grant',"$100,000 to Sussex University's Centre for German-Jewish Studies, 1996"],
      ['Note','Contemporary RPF grantmaking is the subject of contested reporting; this entry covers the founding act']
    ],
    body: [
      `For Steven Spielberg, Schindler's List was never only a film. He has said the making of it changed him, that Jewish life came pouring back into his heart on that set, that he cried through much of the shoot. It was a reckoning, and the refusal of the money was part of the reckoning, not a footnote to it. The profit, he said, had to go back out into the world.`,
      `So in 1994 it did. Spielberg put his entire share into a new body, the Righteous Persons Foundation, which by its own count has given more than a hundred and sixty million dollars in the three decades since, from a hundred thousand dollars to a German-Jewish studies center at the University of Sussex in 1996 to a thirty-million-dollar endowment delivered to the USC Shoah Foundation in late 2024. That second institution he founded the same year, and it now holds the largest archive of Holocaust survivor testimony in the world. Two institutions, born of one film and one conscience, in a single year.`,
      `For honesty's sake, the Ledger notes that the foundation's recent grantmaking has drawn contested coverage, much of it from opinion and advocacy outlets, with figures that have not been independently audited. That dispute is not this entry's subject, and the reader who wishes to follow it can. What is not in dispute is the founding act, and what it set in motion: a filmmaker who would not profit from his people's destruction, and turned that refusal into thirty years of building.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"It is blood money. Let's call it what it is. I didn't take a single dollar from the profits I received from Schindler's List because I did consider it blood money."`, attr: 'Steven Spielberg, TODAY with Katie Couric (NBC), 2004', color: 'gold' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-film', label: 'Our Take', text: `Most of the giving in this Ledger is institutional: a studio's gift, a production's surplus, a set left standing. This one was a person's reckoning. A Jewish filmmaker who had been beaten as a child for being Jewish made a film about the murder of his own people, and decided, before a frame was shot, that he could not keep a dollar of what it earned. He called it blood money and meant it literally. What makes the act belong at the top of the Filmanthropic record is not the foundation that followed, remarkable as it is. It is that the giving was settled at the moment of decision, as a condition of the work rather than a reward for it. That is what designed-in looks like when conscience, not strategy, does the designing.` }
  },
  'slumdog': {
    bCls: 'badge-film', bLabel: 'Filmanthropic',
    meta: 'Fox Searchlight · 2008 · Dir. Danny Boyle',
    name: 'Slumdog Millionaire',
    editorial: 'The Trust That Could Only Do So Much',
    stats: [
      ['Donation','$747,500 to the charity Plan (Feb 2009, before the Oscars)'],
      ['Stated goal','Educate 5,000 Mumbai slum children over five years'],
      ['For the two child actors',"The Jai Ho Trust, named for the film's song, set up by Danny Boyle and producer Christian Colson"],
      ['Trust terms','Schooling, housing held in trust, a monthly stipend tied to 70% attendance, assets transferred at age 18; three trustees'],
      ['Beneficiaries','Rubina Ali and Azharuddin Mohammed Ismail, found in the Mumbai slums for the film'],
      ['2009 disruptions',"Both families' slum homes demolished in city clearances; a now-defunct tabloid's allegation against Ali's father was denied, unevidenced, never charged"],
      ['Rubina Ali, now','Studied fashion design; opened a beauty salon (2022); married (Nov 2023)'],
      ['Azharuddin Ismail, now','Left school; a business failed; sold the trust apartment (~2019) and returned to slum, then village, life'],
      ['Source','AP, Feb 2009; trust outcome per Mumbai Mirror, 2020, and later reporting']
    ],
    body: [
      `The giving here was not improvised after the Oscars. In February 2009, before the Academy Awards the film would go on to sweep, Danny Boyle and the producers announced a donation of seven hundred and forty-seven thousand dollars to the children's charity Plan, aimed at educating five thousand Mumbai slum children over five years. Boyle put it simply: a film that had taken so much from the city owed something back to it.`,
      `The more intimate instrument was the Jai Ho Trust, named for the film's Oscar-winning song and set up for the two child actors the production had found in the slums, Rubina Ali and Azharuddin Mohammed Ismail. It was a careful design: school fees, an apartment held in trust for each child, a monthly stipend that the trustees could cut if attendance fell below seventy percent, and the assets signed over to each child at eighteen. Three trustees were appointed to hold the line. By the standard of how productions usually treat children cast out of real poverty, who are usually thanked and forgotten, this was rare and serious. The filmmakers understood that the film had used these children's actual lives as its material, and that something was owed.`,
      `What the trust could not govern was everything around it. In May 2009, weeks after the film won eight Academy Awards, Mumbai authorities bulldozed the slum shanties both families lived in, part of a clearance drive, and the cruelty of the timing drew the world's press to their doorsteps. A British tabloid, since shut down, ran a sting alleging that Ali's father would sell her; he denied it, the police found no evidence, and no charge was ever brought, but the story chased the family anyway. The father died of tuberculosis in 2020. The attention that the film brought was supposed to be a blessing. Much of it landed as a weight.`,
      `The trust held to its terms. When each child turned eighteen, the apartments were handed over and the trust was closed, exactly as designed. From there the two lives moved apart. Rubina Ali kept her footing: she studied fashion design, opened a beauty salon near Mumbai in 2022, and married in 2023, a modest and self-made adulthood, not the stardom the headlines once imagined, but hers. Azharuddin Mohammed Ismail did not find the same ground. He left school, a business venture failed, and around 2019 he sold the apartment the trust had given him and moved back into a slum, and later to his family's village. The same gift, structured the same way, carried one child to steadiness and could not hold the other.`,
      `Neither outcome was the trust's to dictate. A trust can buy an apartment and pay a school. It cannot reach into a family, a neighborhood, or the gravity of poverty that pulls at a child long after the cameras leave. The production did more than almost any production does, and it was still not enough to settle how two lives would turn out. That is not an argument against giving carefully. It is the reason giving has to be designed not only into the production, but into the years that follow it, with people whose job is to stay.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"Having benefited so much from the hospitality of the people of Mumbai it is only right that some of the success of the movie be plowed back into the city."`, attr: 'Danny Boyle, Director, Associated Press, February 2009', color: 'gold' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-film', label: 'Our Take', text: `This is not a story about a production that failed to give. The giving was real and unusually careful: a donation to educate thousands of Mumbai children, and a trust built to carry two of them, the child actors found in the slums, into safe and schooled adult lives. Most productions in this position give nothing. These filmmakers thought hard about the gift. And still, years later, the two children it was built for had arrived at opposite ends of the same hope. One built a modest, steady life. The other sold what the trust had given him and went back to where the film had found him. The trust did everything a trust can do. What it could not do was reach past the apartment and the school fees into the family, the neighborhood, and the poverty that were always going to decide the rest. That is the hard lesson of this case, and the Ledger keeps it in: giving designed in at the production level is necessary, and it is not always enough.` }
  },
  'braddock': {
    bCls: 'badge-film', bLabel: 'Filmanthropic',
    meta: 'Pennsylvania · 2009–2014 · Multiple Productions',
    name: 'Braddock Free Store',
    editorial: 'The Proof of Demand',
    stats: [
      ['Location','Braddock, Pennsylvania, a steel town outside Pittsburgh'],
      ['Operator',"Gisele Fetterman, founder of the Free Store (2012); then wife of Braddock's mayor, John Fetterman, later U.S. Senator"],
      ['Productions that donated',"Several; by WPXI's 2014 count, the Fathers &amp; Daughters gift was the fifth (the earlier four are not named in the record)"],
      ['Best-documented donation',"Fathers &amp; Daughters (Russell Crowe drama): ~400 boxes of children's clothing, furniture, and set items (WPXI, May 2014)"],
      ['Physical setup','Repurposed shipping containers'],
      ['The pattern','Each film donation was ad hoc, arranged when an individual made the connection; no standing program linked productions to the store'],
      ['Context',"Pittsburgh-area film activity rose and fell with Pennsylvania's film tax credit; production traffic is transient"],
      ['Still operating','Yes, as Free Store 15104 (freestore15104.org), confirmed active 2025']
    ],
    body: [
      `Braddock is a steel town outside Pittsburgh that lost most of its industrial base across the late twentieth century, and in the years when western Pennsylvania's film tax credit drew productions to the region, it often stood in as the backdrop for stories about working-class American decline. Several of those productions, when they wrapped, gave their surplus to a small local operation that handed it straight to people who needed it: the Free Store, founded in 2012 by Gisele Fetterman, then the wife of Braddock's mayor, John Fetterman, and run out of repurposed shipping containers.`,
      `The best-documented of those gifts came in May 2014. When the crew of Fathers and Daughters, the Russell Crowe drama, finished shooting around Pittsburgh, they delivered roughly four hundred boxes of children's clothing, along with furniture and an entire set's worth of material, to the Free Store. A volunteer, Shiane Prunty, described keeping the boxes moving as fast as families could empty them. Fetterman's point about it was simple and the opposite of how this usually goes: the production had found a way to turn what a film leaves behind into something useful, rather than letting it be destroyed.`,
      `By the local station's count, that was the fifth time a production had given its surplus to the store. The earlier four are not named in the record, which is itself part of the lesson: each donation happened because a particular person, on a particular wrap day, thought to make the call. There was no standing arrangement, no role whose job was to ensure it happened, nothing that would catch the next production automatically. The giving was real every time. It was also, every time, a coincidence of the right person being in the right place.`,
      `That is why it did not last. Film production in the region rises and falls with the tax credit that draws it, and when the productions came through town less often, the donations thinned with them. The Free Store itself survived, and operates still as Free Store 15104. The production pipeline into it did not, because nothing had been built to hold it open. This is the exact problem the Forerunners solved in their own corners: Mandelbaum with a contractual rider, the reuse operators with standing departments and warehouses. Braddock proves the demand is there and the generosity is there. What it shows, by their absence, is how much the mechanism matters, and why a field like this one has to be designed, not left to whoever happens to be in the room.`
    ],
    quote: { text: `"I think they found another way now to take these items and do something great with them."`, attr: 'Gisele Fetterman, founder, the Free Store, WPXI, May 2014', color: 'gold' },
    verdict: { cls: 'verdict-film', label: 'Our Take', text: `Braddock is the proof of demand. The productions were willing. The community need was real and right there. By a local station's count, surplus from several films reached the Free Store, each time because one person happened to make the connection at the right moment. What was missing was the thing the Forerunners spent decades building: a coordinating layer that makes the giving reliable and repeatable, so it does not depend on the right person being in the right room on the right day. When the productions stopped coming through town, the donations stopped with them, while the need stayed exactly where it was. That gap, between a generosity that clearly exists and a mechanism to make it dependable, is the whole reason this field has to be built on purpose.` }
  }
};
